WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.640
- So, good afternoon, everyone,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.140
and thank you for being here,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.300
and it's really a pleasure to welcome you

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.140
to this semester's Women of Yale lecture.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.860
We began this series a couple of years ago

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.820
to showcase the accomplishments of women

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.100
who graduated from Yale,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.923
and our guests, including our
distinguished speaker today,

00:00:32.121 --> 00:00:37.121
Quiara Hudes, represent the
very best of this university,

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.340
and they reflect the remarkable success

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.803
of coeducation at Yale.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.333
My wife Marta Moret, who
is also a woman of Yale,

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:53.310
a 1984 graduate of our
public health school,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.910
she's actually the who
first proposed the idea

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.190
of a speaker series that
would bring back to Yale

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.250
some of the most inspiring women

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.000
and to do it in this year,
or leading up to this year,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.630
the year in which we
celebrate the 50th anniversary

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.300
of coeducation in Yale College.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.440
And it's been a fantastic
way to meet and hear

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.723
from some of our most brilliant
and accomplished alumni.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.920
These events have
allowed us to commemorate

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.230
the legacy of women at this university,

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.600
to learn about their leadership,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.420
their contributions to society,

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.100
and you'll recall our
first speaker was Maya Lin,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.120
a pioneering architect who graduated

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.530
from Yale College in 1981,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.600
earned her masters degree from
the School of Architecture

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.390
in 1986,

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.960
and returned a year later to receive

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.440
an honorary doctor of fine
arts degree from Yale.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.270
After that, we welcomed Vera
Wells, who is here today.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.840
Sitting right here.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.290
She was a member of the class of 1971,

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.520
the first Yale College graduating
class to include women,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.630
and she was the recipient
of the Yale Medal

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.910
for service to the university.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:11.910
Then we honored Anita Hill,
a lawyer, trailblazer,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.280
scholar, and advocate for women's rights.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.010
She graduated from the
Yale Law School in 1980.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:21.920
And then last year,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.270
we heard from Dr. Patricia Nez Henderson.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.160
She is the first Native American woman

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.040
to graduate from the
Yale School of Medicine

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.240
and she earned her masters
degree in public health

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.483
from Yale as well.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.700
So, as we celebrate these women pioneers,

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.670
we need to remember that actually,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.090
women have been at Yale as students

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.810
longer than the 50 years in Yale College.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:48.960
The School of Art,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.700
when it first opened
its doors 150 years ago,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.440
women and men studied together,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.440
so it's fitting that we
welcome today Quiara Hudes

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:05.020
who was a 1999 graduate of Yale College,

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.230
but is a playwright,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.190
an author from West Philly

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.610
with roots in Puerto Rico and Boriqua,

00:03:11.982 --> 00:03:16.982
and she shows us, because she
studies it and practices it,

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:21.963
the importance of arts in our world.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.310
Her plays and musicals, I don't
have to tell you about them.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.730
They've been performed around the world.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.770
They include "Elliot: A Soldier's Fugue,"

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.957
a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize,

00:03:31.957 --> 00:03:33.380
"Water by the Spoonful,"

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.580
winner of the 2012
Pulitzer Prize for drama,

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.050
and the Tony Award-winning
Broadway hit "In the Heights."

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:45.050
We were actually talking
before this program

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.860
about our family.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:47.790
She lives in the Heights

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.130
and I have an uncle
who was in the Heights.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.100
My nephew lives in the Heights,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.913
and so sharing tales of the neighborhood.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.470
Charles Isherwood of the New York Times

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.967
described "In the Heights"
as "a panoramic portrait

00:04:01.967 --> 00:04:03.807
"of a New York neighborhood filled

00:04:03.807 --> 00:04:07.677
"with Spanish-speaking
Dreamers of American dreams,

00:04:07.677 --> 00:04:11.777
"nervously eying their
futures from a city block

00:04:11.777 --> 00:04:13.467
"on the cusp of change."

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.620
Ms. Hudes has adapted her
award-winning work for the screen,

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.710
and it is in release this summer, right?

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.433
At movie theaters all across the country.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.610
Her most recent musical,
"Miss You Like Hell,"

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.200
ran off-Broadway at the Public Theater,

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.710
and she has written a memoir

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.150
which will be published next year

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.513
by One World, Random House.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.380
She's the co-founder
of Emancipated Stories,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.210
a platform for incarcerated
people to share their stories

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.767
through writing and art,

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:50.690
and we are so grateful that
you are here with us today.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.750
So grateful that you
share your story with us.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.480
Today, she will speak to us
about art and disruption,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.189
and I ask you to join me
in welcoming Quiara Hudes.

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:06.384
(audience applauding)

00:05:06.384 --> 00:05:08.020
Thank you for doing this today.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:08.853
I appreciate it.

00:05:08.853 --> 00:05:11.716
(audience applauding)

00:05:11.716 --> 00:05:13.359
Take mine.

00:05:13.359 --> 00:05:14.359
- All right.

00:05:15.341 --> 00:05:16.890
Hello.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.160
- [Audience] Hello.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.237
- How y'all doing?

00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:20.160
- [Audience] Good.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.353
- Um.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.100
I'm tempted to start by going off-script

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.600
and see who would be willing,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.080
let's get a little physical for a second,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.590
so two parts to this

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.850
because this is gonna
be like a bedtime story

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:39.683
that I'm about to do,

00:05:39.683 --> 00:05:42.280
even though it's before
everyone's bedtime, but...

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.200
So, it's hard to give a
bedtime story that far back,

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.750
so if anyone feels compelled

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:51.500
to come a little bit closer to me

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.970
so I can kind of like look you in the eye

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.670
while telling this bedtime
story, I would welcome that.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.030
No pressure, if you're
comfortable, stay where you are,

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.600
but if you want to come up so
I can really connect with you,

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.853
the closer the better.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.140
So, I'll give you a moment to do that,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.430
but then I'm gonna ask everyone,
also, wherever you are,

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.913
to get a little physical.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:18.040
Ooh, it means people are
sitting closer to each other.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.240
I like this.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.070
And I'm gonna be doing a Q&amp;A
at the end of my remarks,

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:26.130
so if anything, any thoughts come up,

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.590
that you're interested in discussing,

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.440
I know there's some students here

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.480
who are interested in
incarceration topics, possibly.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.650
You know, just things that
come to mind, just flag it,

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:36.500
because we'll have time

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.803
to open up the conversation at the end.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.923
Um, okay, so,

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:44.620
I think before I start,

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.270
I learned this from an
activist I met in Philadelphia.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.570
I'm gonna turn off my mic.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:55.863
Just to connect us in the room,

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.220
I'm gonna ask everyone
to start by screaming,

00:07:02.147 --> 00:07:03.400
not so that you hurt your throat,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.590
not so that you hurt your neighbor,

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:10.590
but just to give the room
our energy as a collective

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.480
to just be energetic in the room together.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:15.880
I know it sounds a little strange.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.290
I'm gonna join you in this scream,

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.340
so...

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:24.160
I think seated is okay.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.520
I don't want to ask y'all to do too much.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.210
But if you'll take the leap of faith,

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.530
it's not gonna blow anyone's ears out.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:31.450
It's actually almost like

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.933
a strange sort of like
prayer or something.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:36.490
Cool?

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.440
You guys trust me enough to give it a try?

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.609
So we're just gonna scream
for a second, all right?

00:07:40.609 --> 00:07:42.980
(audience laughing)

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:44.160
All right, ready?

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.010
Here we go.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.697
One, two, three.

00:07:47.697 --> 00:07:50.364
(all screaming)

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:54.303
Whew, thank you.

00:07:54.303 --> 00:07:56.016
(audience laughing)

00:07:56.016 --> 00:07:57.365
All right, let me turn my mic back on.

00:07:57.365 --> 00:07:59.200
(audience laughing)

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.593
Okay, so here we go.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:05.360
Wait, am I, okay.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.960
Got a lot of mics.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.870
So, so this thing I'm about to do

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.930
called "Art and Disruption" is based

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:15.420
on years of conversations
with Corey Menafee

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.990
here in New Haven over
the last four years,

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.820
but mostly in 2016.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.610
I paraphrased just a tiny amount,

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.617
and the only editing is
for clarity and continuity.

00:08:27.617 --> 00:08:30.380
Corey's here and I want to thank him

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.870
from the bottom of my heart.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.930
The rest of my presentation is
pulled right out of my life,

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:38.453
so I took liberties with
that, but it's all true.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.570
Also, shouting out Andrea Olivares

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.590
who designed my PowerPoint slides.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.410
It's mostly art from children's
books that she messed with,

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:51.223
that she broke or disrupted,
so, art and disruption.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.130
All right, so I thought I'd tell a tale,

00:08:56.130 --> 00:09:01.070
spin a yarn, kind of like
circle time, story hour,

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:04.810
a night-night book about an ordinary guy

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.610
and an ordinary girl who came
of age in the '80s and '90s

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.533
listening to some of the tunes
you heard while walking in.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.180
He from the African American
Hill section of New Haven,

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.020
she from West Philly and
North Philly, el barrio,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.473
Puerto Rico and Philadelphia.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.993
And a moment that they
crossed paths at Yale.

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:29.680
I even went Aesop-style and wrote a moral

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.300
at the end of the story.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.047
So, that was actually my prologue.

00:09:34.047 --> 00:09:34.880
(audience laughing)

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.400
Let's go.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:37.233
Okay.

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:42.720
Once upon a time,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.470
a child named Corey
lived on Orchard Street

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.940
but would hang on Kensington.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.740
Then he moved to Sheffield
Ave, then Lake Place,

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.730
where his family had more room.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:56.600
Every three to five years, Corey
moved to a different house.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.130
On those New Haven streets,

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.510
Corey would ride bikes, play basketball,

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.250
get up with his buddies.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.370
Young, carefree, going with the flow.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.060
At four or five or six,

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.270
he took to the New York Giants

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.360
because blue was his favorite color

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.460
or because he was watching
with his grandfather

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.640
and older uncles who were all Giants fans.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.170
Either way, he loved all
teams that wore blue.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.240
Michigan football, The
Mets, Hillhouse High.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.403
He always rooted for Hillhouse over Cross.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.260
For winter holidays, his
family split into two sides.

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.980
On one side, his mother's
mother's branch, was,

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:34.130
how to put this?

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.340
Middle class.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.380
You wore your good shirt and tie.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.220
You sat around the
table and said a prayer,

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.840
and if there was music
playing, it was violins.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.800
You ate Thanksgiving or Christmas dinner

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:47.363
at one or two in the afternoon.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.580
Then he would go to the
Menafee side, to Powpow's,

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:54.390
his father's father was Powpow.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:55.990
That side was more urban.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:57.870
He'd put on his jeans and hoodie.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.880
There was gonna be loud music,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.880
dancing, playing spades,

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:04.293
yelling and screaming over the moves.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:08.790
On July 4th, they would
get up before sunrise

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.313
and go to Wharton Brook, both
sides of the family together.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.610
Corey would stay guarding the picnic table

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:19.030
while the elders parked
and unpacked the food.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.890
There was always a deck of
cards for playing spades.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:23.430
Running back and forth to the beach,

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.170
rolling down dirt hills,

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.880
that's how Corey passed the
time with the other kids,

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:30.890
stuff that now he's
like, man, that was dumb.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:35.193
His two grandmothers plus Aunt
Gladys cooking the day away.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:39.210
His mom was on macaroni-and-cheese duty,

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.210
Powpow worked the grill,

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.300
there would always be a pot of rice,

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.280
cabbage or string beans,

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.460
chicken or pork chops, a baked ham,

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.390
sweet potatoes,

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.763
cornbread, lasagna, the whole works.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:56.750
After his mom's vision went,

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.420
Corey became her eyes in the kitchen.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.460
That's how he learned to cook.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.780
Elbows, extra sharp
cheddar, a stick of butter,

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.450
two eggs, American cheese.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.330
But to this day,

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:13.250
his mac and cheese never
comes out quite the same.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:14.853
The love she put into it.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:17.890
Caring and sympathetic.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:19.660
Nice, kindhearted,

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:22.700
but she'd cuss your
ass out in a heartbeat.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.560
A strong, spirited woman.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.373
That was his mom's personality.

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:29.270
He became independent like her,

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:32.073
not liking to depend
on nobody for nothing.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.630
History and social studies
were his best subjects

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:38.020
because it's a matter of memorization.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.490
You just cram the facts into your brain.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:43.290
The school laid out two possible paths.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:46.490
You could be like W.E.B.
DuBois who believed

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:48.940
that black people should
take on professional roles

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.353
like doctor, professor, lawyer.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.730
They should use their mind to give back,

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:58.550
a mindset Corey would embrace
years later at college.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.440
Or you could be a Booker
T. Washington guy,

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.790
cast your buckets down here.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:05.420
Learn a trade.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.500
Gain practical skills.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.410
After college in his twenties,

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.313
he would become a Booker T. Guy.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:16.280
Corey, it was said, possessed
an ability Powpow had.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:19.920
He could walk with kings and bums alike,

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.730
plus he got Powpow's comedic intelligence.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.490
Being in a bad mood around Corey was hard.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.267
In second grade, the teacher
called his mom and said,

00:13:28.267 --> 00:13:29.657
"Corey does his work.

00:13:29.657 --> 00:13:31.497
"He's a good student.

00:13:31.497 --> 00:13:33.507
"But he's the class clown.

00:13:33.507 --> 00:13:35.147
"He likes to goof around."

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:40.110
Well, his mom came into school
and whooped him right there

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.410
with all the kids laughing.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.540
She gave away his
lunchbox and his backpack.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.350
She and Corey lived
with Powpow at the time

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:48.810
and he kicked her out for a minute.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:52.413
He was mad, but she had high
expectations of her son.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.540
When Corey was 10, his mom got pregnant.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:59.220
As a diabetic, she was
put on strict bedrest.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.740
It was on Corey to do things now.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.100
Push a cart full of clothes down the hill,

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:07.490
learn how to operate a washing
machine at the laundromat,

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.810
bring the list of groceries
and money to the store

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.600
and do the shopping.

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:14.597
When she went into labor, the doc said,

00:14:14.597 --> 00:14:16.750
"This baby's gonna die, too."

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:20.773
See, Corey's sister had been
premature and died at birth.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:26.120
His brother, William, also
premature, died at birth,

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.310
but this time the docs were wrong.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:30.520
Corey became a big brother,

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:32.150
the love his life.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:34.270
That's what Mitchell became.

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:36.410
Corey fed him, changed him,

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:39.610
handed him off to a girl
to go play street ball.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:42.767
His mom would say, "I had
Mitchell just for you."

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:45.837
But if Corey was bad, she'd make threats.

00:14:45.837 --> 00:14:46.837
"You're out of my will.

00:14:46.837 --> 00:14:48.105
"You're not getting Mitch."

00:14:48.105 --> 00:14:50.070
(audience laughing)

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.220
Seventh grade.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.610
Girls began to develop.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:57.220
Corey fell in love with a
different one every day.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:59.320
A girl would randomly smile, say hello,

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:02.209
and Corey would think,
oh my God, I love her.

00:15:02.209 --> 00:15:03.042
(audience laughing)

00:15:03.042 --> 00:15:06.550
He had no skills whatsoever
but high aspirations,

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.830
and the girls that liked him,
he didn't like them back,

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:11.193
so he went to the eighth
grade dance alone.

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.603
In high school, he was a brain.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.953
Biggie Smalls,

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:23.760
Wu-Tang, that's what he liked.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.220
His Hillside friends let him
hang but pushed him aside

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.750
when it came to extremes.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:30.540
Drugs, beefing.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:33.377
The Trey guys, they'd always
be be beefing with Hillside.

00:15:33.377 --> 00:15:36.030
"Nah, this ain't for
you," his friends said.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.380
They could have seduced him into it,

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.100
but they had that type of love for him.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.090
But once, walking home
at one in the morning,

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.590
he ran into some guys he knew.

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:46.900
A Trey guy was like, "Where you from?"

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.660
Corey said, "The hill."

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:49.793
Guy had a gun on him.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.900
Said, "You should run."

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.150
But Corey, being young
and cocky, was like,

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.550
I ain't gonna run.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.373
Just walking away slowly.

00:15:59.543 --> 00:16:00.780
Shots started going off.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.920
One ricocheted off his shoe.

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:05.120
This guy was shooting
everything but Corey,

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:06.820
then was like, "Turn around, turn around,"

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:07.970
and when Corey did,

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:10.550
he put that gun right up on Corey's chest.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:11.930
Click.

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:14.090
He had run out of bullets.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.800
Later that night, Corey
was just thanking God,

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:20.330
counting the bullet holes in his pants.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.563
The style was real baggy back then.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:26.870
It dawned on Corey, he
really tried to shoot me.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.350
Duh.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.500
That's how he learned
don't go 'round saying

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:31.940
you're from any neighborhood

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.790
since he didn't have something
on his hip to protect him.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.850
Instead of beefing, he
went to play practice,

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:43.600
cast as Atticus Finch in
"To Kill a Mockingbird,"

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.610
missing rehearsal only
for football practice,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.350
even though he stunk.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.837
To quote him, "I had a mixed skillset

00:16:50.837 --> 00:16:53.820
"that didn't relate to
success on the field."

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:55.730
He could catch, but he wasn't fast.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:59.290
He was a very undersized
offensive lineman.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.820
Anyone after two or three years of this,

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.320
going to practice every day faithfully,

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:07.460
not getting any play
time, would have quit.

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:08.390
But Corey stuck with it.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:09.463
He loved football.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:15.510
Later in life, if he said
he played in high school,

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:17.007
his uncle would correct him.

00:17:17.007 --> 00:17:18.816
"You were on the team."

00:17:18.816 --> 00:17:21.130
(audience laughing)

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.330
Senior prom, this time he had a date.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:26.197
Last dance of the night was Keith Sweat,

00:17:26.197 --> 00:17:27.467
"Make it Last Forever."

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:32.350
College tuition would be tight.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.020
His mother had been a home health aide,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.040
what they called private duty.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:38.940
She had worked 11 through
seven then at a factory,

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:40.780
but she became disabled,

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.330
the result of juvenile diabetes.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:45.700
She lost a toe, went blind,

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.280
seeing only blurry shapes and light.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:49.840
With no money in his pocket,

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:53.540
Corey headed down to
Virginia Union University,

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:55.760
stole books from the bookstore,

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.860
went homeless for an entire semester,

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:00.590
meaning he was an off-campus student,

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:03.120
meaning he couldn't
eat in the dining hall,

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.810
so he would steal from grocery stores,

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.303
things he wasn't proud of.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:11.580
But, the journalism major
taught him to write,

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:13.180
to choose a subject,

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:17.060
to qualify the story and
talk to both parties,

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:18.903
tell it from both aspects.

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.610
Graduation came in the early aughts,

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:24.780
the same year his mom died.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:26.230
The night before her passing,

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:29.260
she coughed and sneezed really hard.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:33.720
She went to the bathroom on herself.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:37.610
Okay, so not number two, just wet.

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:40.060
He said, "I have to change you."

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.820
She resisted, wanted to rest.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:44.570
He said, "All right."

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:47.010
Next morning, she was unresponsive.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:51.780
Sugar level almost on a coma,
Corey called an ambulance,

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:55.763
and while they came, he
changed his mother's underwear.

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.200
That's a very hard thing for a man to do.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.810
No man wants to change
his mother's underwear,

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:05.410
but she couldn't go to the
emergency room like that.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.260
Four years.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.920
That's how long it took
Corey to read the Bible,

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:13.300
cover to cover.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:15.710
He set a goal and accomplished it.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:18.520
Finished in June of 2015,

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.670
admittedly, he skipped
around a little bit.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:26.063
His favorite verse was Philippians
Chapter Four, Verse 13.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.183
Anyone know that one?

00:19:30.267 --> 00:19:32.467
"I can do all things through Christ,

00:19:32.467 --> 00:19:35.240
"for it is he who strengthens me."

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:37.000
He told me you can read something

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.070
and you can even comprehend
what it's saying,

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:42.190
but until it applies to
your life, you don't know.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:45.070
For example, he was going
over to Whitney Avenue

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.800
to bid on a Yale dining hall job.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:50.240
He had to be at work
at Davenport by 11:30,

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.750
but by the time he
finished his bid paperwork,

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:54.980
it was already 11:15.

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:58.740
Mind you, he walks with a limp
from a long-ago car accident,

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:00.860
and he was like, how am I gonna get there?

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:03.947
So, he recited the verse
and started walking.

00:20:03.947 --> 00:20:06.057
"I can do all things through Christ,

00:20:06.057 --> 00:20:08.277
"for it is he who strengthens me."

00:20:08.277 --> 00:20:10.563
"I can do all things through Christ,

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:13.133
"for it is he who strengthens me."

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:18.303
He swiped in at 11:30 precisely.

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:21.270
From the get-go,

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.130
when his mother-in-law
introduced him to her manager,

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:27.700
the Yale job felt like
family and the pay was good.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:29.070
Take out the trash,

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:30.510
work in the dish room,

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.320
keep the floors clean, compost,

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:36.980
that is, separate the food
waste from the other trash.

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:38.650
Sometimes doing the same tasks

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:40.710
for weeks and weeks gets stale,

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:43.560
so every once in a while, the
workers would switch it up.

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:49.140
Often, they shared a family meal,

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.570
sat down together and ate the
food that they had cooked.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:56.070
Healthy meals, vegan dishes, a salad bar,

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:58.583
and Corey, being diabetic,
that worked for him.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.870
The backbone of Yale University.

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.780
That's how he described his coworkers.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:08.030
Without staff and those behind the scenes,

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.087
the university couldn't thrive.

00:21:10.087 --> 00:21:11.957
"There'd be chaos if
the students came to eat

00:21:11.957 --> 00:21:14.130
"and we weren't there," Corey said,

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.920
and he loved the work.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:18.340
One simple, hi, how you doing?

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.990
Could mean a big difference to a student.

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:24.480
Seeing people every day, they opened up.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.510
He was invited to a
gospel choir performance

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:28.140
at Battell Chapel.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:30.900
Tired at the end of his
shift, he wanted to go home.

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.270
Instead, after a hard day of work,

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:35.800
the gospel choir was refreshing.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.620
The girl who invited him was in tears,

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.847
told him, "I view you as a father figure."

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.900
He knew some of these students
were thousands of miles away

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.560
from everyone they knew and loved.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:50.380
He remembered being in their
shoes at Virginia Union

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:53.180
that they might not have
anyone around to reassure them,

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:57.300
so that being able to open up, say,

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:00.937
yeah, it's been a hard
week, might help them.

00:22:00.937 --> 00:22:02.977
"We have a lot of fun
at work," Corey told me,

00:22:02.977 --> 00:22:05.417
"but our main concern is
to attend to students,

00:22:05.417 --> 00:22:08.410
"and I take that very seriously," he said.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:13.410
Oh, and one more detail before
I end Part one of this story.

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:17.460
When Corey was 16, Miss
Moñelo, his Spanish teacher,

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:19.370
held him after class.

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:22.467
She said, "Corey, you're a straight kid.

00:22:22.467 --> 00:22:24.357
"You follow the rules.

00:22:24.357 --> 00:22:27.750
"One day, you're gonna have
to learn how to break a rule."

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.970
His mother always instilled
do the right thing,

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.450
and now an authority figure was like

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:33.260
learn to break a rule.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:34.453
His mind was blown.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:41.293
Once upon a time, there
was a place called Yale.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:46.300
Beautiful buildings were
connected by stone paths,

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:50.360
cast-iron gates, and border
walls of sturdy masonry.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:53.093
Each room was full of many curious hearts,

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:57.010
souls hungry to learn.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:23:00.160
Yale owned so many gorgeous
buildings and artworks

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:03.170
that one might say
beauty was its property.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:06.970
Sterling Library alone had
$2,000 unique stained glass

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:08.820
or printed glass panels,

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.340
fitting for an institution
whose Latin motto meant

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:12.453
truth and light.

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:16.410
Reunion weekend, 2016.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.733
A Sunday in May, 11:30 a.m.

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:23.940
The Calhoun dining staff had
completed its work for the day,

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:26.260
cleaning up after the reunions ended.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:29.340
Corey was thinking, should I
leave now and take half a day?

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:31.940
Because the shift was
supposed to end at three.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:35.280
He thought, well, stay till
12 to make it an even number.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.940
That's when the knock came at the door.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:38.950
No one wanted to open it.

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:40.590
They had already finished up.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.310
But Corey relented.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:44.850
Standing there was an African American man

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.330
alongside a girl of about 10.

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:51.507
The stranger said, "Hi, I
used to be a student here.

00:23:51.507 --> 00:23:54.100
"Can I show my daughter
where I used to eat?"

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:55.400
Corey let him in.

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:57.300
There in the empty dining hall,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.210
the alumnus started telling his daughter

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:01.460
about the removal of names,

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:03.080
how in his undergrad years,

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:05.500
African American students like himself

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:08.233
wanted the name Calhoun
removed from the college.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:12.080
Explaining to his daughter
how John C. Calhoun

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:16.040
grew up in the late 1700s
believing what his daddy told him,

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:18.790
that slavery was a moral good,

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:22.460
traveling from the
South up to attend Yale,

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:24.870
how in the U.S. Senate
he opposed abolition

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:29.560
and passionately led the
pro-slavery legislative agenda,

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:32.030
views he took the White
House as Secretary of State,

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:34.040
then Vice President.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:36.890
Father told daughter these things.

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:39.380
In this empty dining hall,
Corey and the workers heard,

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:41.340
and at times joined in.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.190
The stranger welcomes this.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:45.960
He explained how in his
undergraduate years,

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:49.040
Yale had removed Calhoun's
stained glass portrait.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.010
Not the whole thing,

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:52.800
just the part where an African
American man in shackles

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.183
knelt at Calhoun's feet.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:57.640
Then, father led daughter to the small,

00:24:57.640 --> 00:24:59.570
stained glass windows.

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:01.720
They went in sequence, he explained.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.420
First, a picture of a tree.

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:06.480
Next, a house.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:10.230
Next, enslaved people picking cotton.

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.650
Corey thought, no, they don't show that.

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:13.940
I've been working here a long time.

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:16.110
I ain't seen nothing like that.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:18.560
But, the father invited
Corey to check it out,

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.540
this window beneath which he
had eaten his college meals.

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:26.020
You see, the windows are
small and a little high up,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:28.260
and Corey's vision is blurry,

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:31.580
just like his mother before
him, also from diabetes.

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:34.353
Corey needed glasses, still does.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:40.070
So, Corey leaned in close and
saw it for the first time.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:43.230
African Americans with
cotton bales on their head,

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:47.210
one of them smiling, looking peaceful.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:49.830
Corey was like, oh, that can't be here.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:53.010
That's not right, no, no, no.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:54.060
Because it was deliberate

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:56.500
that these were African American people,

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:58.623
one with a smile on her face.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.110
After that when Corey came into
work, it was staring at him,

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:05.510
eating away at his subconscious,

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:07.080
like you think someone's watching you,

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.380
that feeling of eyes on your
back, eyes on your shoulders,

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:11.653
eyes on your neck.

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:16.703
A week later, Corey thought,
that thing has to come down.

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.230
It was God's work.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:21.440
Not planned or premeditated.

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:22.980
All he wanted was to get a paycheck

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.850
and take care of his family,

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:27.020
but instead, he went
and found a broomstick,

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:29.913
climbed up on a heater, and
smashed the window panel.

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:35.010
27 pieces of glass fell
into the moat outside,

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:38.370
the grassy part separating
the wall from the sidewalk.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:43.210
Inside, an old lady said, "Ooh, ooh!"

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:44.690
The production manager was like, dude,

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:47.120
you just destroyed Yale's property.

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:49.418
Corey said, "It looks a lot better now."

00:26:49.418 --> 00:26:50.350
(audience laughing)

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:52.500
Then headed for the locker room.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:55.570
When he was scruffy,
people often disengaged,

00:26:55.570 --> 00:26:58.410
but when he was clean-shaven
and made eye contact,

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.350
people would say hi first.

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.140
So, he went and shaved

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:06.100
as the authorities searched
the building for him.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:08.600
A coworker came into the locker room like,

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:10.670
our general manager is looking for you.

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:12.457
Corey said, "I bet she is."

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:15.960
The authorities questioned him.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:18.450
He was composed, honest.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:20.847
Corey told me, "I'm a standup person.

00:27:20.847 --> 00:27:22.737
"I really despise liars.

00:27:22.737 --> 00:27:25.640
"I don't understand why
people lie all the time."

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.400
He didn't try to cover anything.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:29.950
He accepted responsibility.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:32.227
The sergeant said, "Yo, son, I'm a have

00:27:32.227 --> 00:27:34.190
"to slap cuffs on you for this."

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.157
Corey put out his wrists, calm, and said,

00:27:36.157 --> 00:27:38.300
"Okay, here you go."

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.320
As the two arresting officers were joined

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:44.363
by 15 more to investigate the
scene, Corey went to jail.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:47.410
Resign from the job.

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:50.180
That was the union rep's advice.

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:52.010
Corey agreed to never pursue Yale

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:54.020
for a hostile work environment.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.140
Yale agreed not to seek reparations

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:57.773
for their destroyed property,

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.740
and for emphasis, I, Quiara,

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:03.500
will add an editorial repeat.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:04.860
Property.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:05.693
Property.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.757
Corey signed his letter
of resignation and said,

00:28:10.757 --> 00:28:13.420
"Okay, I'll move on with my life."

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:15.240
He didn't want a felony
hanging over his head

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:16.433
for breaking a window.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:19.380
The rest is a matter of public record,

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:21.990
court record, and Googleable.

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.320
He lost his job, was
charged with a felony,

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:27.290
given a promise to appear,

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:30.080
released after three hours in custody.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.390
His family was all down on him.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:36.930
His baby brother Mitch was like,

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:38.650
how could you be so stupid

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:42.220
to let a picture on the wall
cause you to lose your job?

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:44.070
Until Corey's phones started ringing.

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:47.970
Area codes from Texas, North Carolina.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:50.420
It's such-and-such from
Fox News, from NBC,

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.270
from Democracy Now!

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:53.740
After the media frenzy,

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:55.820
he went from being the
biggest dumb-ass in the family

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:58.172
to maybe it was good that you did that.

00:28:58.172 --> 00:28:59.360
(audience laughing)

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:02.550
Still, they wanted Corey to do two things.

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:05.250
One, offer a letter of apology,

00:29:05.250 --> 00:29:08.870
two, cry his soul out
and beg for his job back.

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:12.470
About the second, he told me, "No."

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:16.370
About the first, he wrote a
five-sentence letter of apology

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:18.830
using these skills he'd let deteriorate

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:20.340
throughout the years,

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:23.830
expressing each sentence, line by line.

00:29:23.830 --> 00:29:27.210
The word choice, the
narrative description.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.750
He hadn't written since college.

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:31.970
There was a big picture
of what he wanted to say.

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:34.140
I'm sorry for what I did.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:37.060
Now, he had five sentences
to paint the picture.

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:38.430
Here's two of them.

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:39.537
Quote.

00:29:39.537 --> 00:29:43.097
"I want to express remorse
for my barbaric actions.

00:29:43.097 --> 00:29:46.600
"Yale College, I truly
do love all of you."

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:48.000
End quote.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:50.440
Off it went to Calhoun College,

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:53.580
but then he wondered, barbaric action?

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:54.820
Really?

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:56.490
Yes and no.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:59.140
If you destroy something, that's barbaric,

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:01.883
and it was never a good
idea to destroy property,

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.910
but if he was in that
situation a million times,

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.693
he'd probably do the same thing.

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:10.070
A cause, a movement,

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:13.170
was already in place that had stagnated.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:15.350
The students had been
protesting for months,

00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:17.410
years, actually, in truth, decades,

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:19.350
to change the name.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:21.130
If Yale didn't listen to the student body,

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.807
why were they gonna listen
to a blue-collar worker?

00:30:23.807 --> 00:30:26.217
"And I promise you," Corey told me,

00:30:26.217 --> 00:30:27.877
"if I had just said something,

00:30:27.877 --> 00:30:30.087
"the image would still be there today."

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:34.710
Soon, Yale dropped the
term Master for a new one,

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:36.330
Head of College.

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:39.893
Soon, Yale renamed
Calhoun College to Hopper.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.113
Corey told me,

00:30:42.113 --> 00:30:44.627
"It makes you wonder why
something so easily resolved

00:30:44.627 --> 00:30:46.837
"becomes so complex.

00:30:46.837 --> 00:30:48.907
"It's almost as though you want the name,

00:30:48.907 --> 00:30:50.740
"the negative buzz."

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:53.430
He told me it was an oppressive image

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:55.973
that a lot of people
felt ill about seeing.

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:00.780
On July 26th, 2016,

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.700
I attended Corey's
second criminal hearing.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:06.990
Out on the courthouse steps,
Black Lives Matter was present.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:09.788
Latino Unidos en Accion
was present, chanting,

00:31:09.788 --> 00:31:13.890
"Change the name, change the name."

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:15.030
Going through security,

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:18.057
I saw one guard handing
out coffees to her crew.

00:31:18.057 --> 00:31:20.047
"What the hell are they
doing out there?" one asked.

00:31:20.047 --> 00:31:21.507
"Man, there's a whole bunch of protesters

00:31:21.507 --> 00:31:22.640
"out there chanting,"

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:23.827
she replied.

00:31:23.827 --> 00:31:27.490
"Hats off in the courtroom,"
the court officer called.

00:31:27.490 --> 00:31:29.610
On one bench, a bug-eyed dude rambled

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:31.800
about his cigarette addiction.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.877
The stenographer complained
about the judge's tardiness.

00:31:34.877 --> 00:31:37.357
"Sometimes he doesn't come
out until after 11," she said.

00:31:37.357 --> 00:31:39.335
"That's why I should have
put my money in the meter."

00:31:39.335 --> 00:31:40.600
(audience laughing)

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:43.730
On another bench, Corey sat, unmoving,

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:45.970
the gait of anticipation.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.720
The docket began.

00:31:47.720 --> 00:31:50.070
First, a second-degree burglary.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.600
Next, a violation of a protective order.

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:54.160
Guilty pleas were logged.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:54.993
So noted.

00:31:54.993 --> 00:31:56.200
So ordered.

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:59.223
And next, all criminal charges
against Corey were dropped.

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:01.520
When Corey walked out,

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:04.720
about half the courtroom
stood and left with him.

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:06.810
Deans and students had
been there all morning

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.680
in quiet support.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:12.190
Outside, people had posters and
banners with his name on it,

00:32:12.190 --> 00:32:16.350
posters that said "Menafee
equals Rosa Parks."

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.177
News cameras and reporters pounced.

00:32:18.177 --> 00:32:20.000
"Do you want your job back?" one asked,

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.047
and a protester shouted,
"No, he doesn't want a job

00:32:22.047 --> 00:32:24.340
"from that racist institution."

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:28.280
Corey went on record then and
there, "I want my job back."

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.577
Soon, Yale went on record, too.

00:32:30.577 --> 00:32:34.040
"If he wants his job back,
we'd love to have him."

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:36.420
That's how his dining hall
position was reinstated,

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:37.483
through the press.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:42.300
That summer, Corey attended the
Hillside reunion like usual.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:44.497
One friend said, "Hey,
man, come over here.

00:32:44.497 --> 00:32:45.527
"Yo, what you did, man,

00:32:45.527 --> 00:32:46.947
"we're gonna give you a clap for that.

00:32:46.947 --> 00:32:49.400
"We're gonna give you some recognition."

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.730
Walking the streets of New Haven,

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:52.570
Corey got nods and thumbs-up from folks,

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:54.320
even from police.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:56.130
Little kids, I want to thank you.

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:57.660
You're a hero.

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:00.037
When he returned to Yale,
a stranger approached.

00:33:00.037 --> 00:33:01.810
"It's good to have you back."

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:03.417
A dining hall manager,

00:33:03.417 --> 00:33:04.667
"I don't agree with what you did,

00:33:04.667 --> 00:33:06.637
"but as a white man, I
respect what you did.

00:33:06.637 --> 00:33:08.770
"I understand why you did it."

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:12.627
A passerby, "Hey, you're
the guy who broke the glass.

00:33:12.627 --> 00:33:14.960
"Oh, man, I appreciate what you did."

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:17.507
A young lady at Stiles
where he now worked,

00:33:17.507 --> 00:33:19.087
"You're so awesome."

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:23.930
And to me, as we sat in a dark bar

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:26.380
having the conversations
that led to this tale,

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:29.827
he said, "Students had
been protesting for months,

00:33:29.827 --> 00:33:32.557
"years, about the Calhoun name.

00:33:32.557 --> 00:33:33.897
"Just break it.

00:33:33.897 --> 00:33:36.647
"You can't live in fear of doing things."

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.380
Once upon a time, (speaks in Spanish),

00:33:45.250 --> 00:33:47.390
there was a girl named Quiara

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:49.653
who grew up in Philly loving art.

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:52.270
Museum art and fancy stuff, sure,

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:54.243
but street art especially.

00:33:55.390 --> 00:33:59.810
She had an aunt who played
neoclassical punk at CBGB

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:03.160
and would sneak her in to
gigs though she was only five.

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:06.150
As her aunt put on thick,
bad-to-the-bone eyeliner,

00:34:06.150 --> 00:34:09.283
the girl was hypnotized by
the vandalized dressing room.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:12.850
There was a neighbor, Ade Mkumba,

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:14.840
who made instruments out of garbage,

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:16.820
performed street concerts by the tracks

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:18.430
on 49th and Baltimore.

00:34:18.430 --> 00:34:22.330
No venue or cover price, just
a kalimba, a thumb piano,

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:24.763
made out of an old, discarded Bustelo can.

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:29.560
Two teenagers dropped a snare
on a West Philly street corner

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:30.970
and started rapping.

00:34:30.970 --> 00:34:34.003
It was catchy, repeated on
loop in the girl's head.

00:34:34.003 --> 00:34:36.615
♪ It looks like another
one's comin' around. ♪

00:34:36.615 --> 00:34:37.695
♪ Pass the what? ♪

00:34:37.695 --> 00:34:39.360
♪ Pass the popcorn." ♪

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.700
Years later, she heard it on Power 99 FM,

00:34:41.700 --> 00:34:42.945
a group called The Roots.

00:34:42.945 --> 00:34:45.070
(audience laughing)

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:47.640
Rennie Harris would
produce Illadelph Legends,

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:51.297
showing schoolkids Mr. Wiggles,
Crazy Legs, Don Campbell.

00:34:51.297 --> 00:34:55.240
They'd be popping, locking,
waacking, voguing, and breaking.

00:34:55.240 --> 00:34:57.530
The girl, like all her classmates,

00:34:57.530 --> 00:34:59.963
got how breaking was creation.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:03.220
Graffiti popped up everywhere,

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:06.640
prettying up broken walls,
uglying up pretty walls.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:08.880
Street legends Karaz and Cornbread,

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:10.470
but the girl didn't know it then.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:12.580
She couldn't make out all the letters,

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:16.233
only that a whole lot of them
read R.I.P. clear as day.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:21.313
Keith Haring defacement became
pins on the girl's backpack.

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:25.870
She saw Kathy Change dance
on the art museum steps

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:28.370
or over by the 38th Street Bridge,

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:31.020
peace signs on her flags and face.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:33.320
Passersby laughed at the dancing fool.

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:35.690
Cops arrested her for breaking the peace.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:37.840
When the dancing fool set herself aflame

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:39.440
before the peace statue,

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:42.320
when she self-immolated
as her final performance,

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:43.513
the girl grieved.

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:49.120
So many after-schools, the girl
walked to West Coast Video,

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:52.640
and rented the same VHS
tape, "Do the Right Thing."

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:54.040
She screamed when Sal

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:57.003
smashed Radio Raheem's boombox to shards.

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:00.100
So that this girl grew up thinking

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:04.270
the purpose of art was
literally to break things now.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.860
Seeing art that often led to arrest,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:09.320
surrounded by art crimes,

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:12.310
it was the '80s and '90s
when crack and AIDS decimated

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:14.120
her abuela's barrio.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:18.030
It was a savage time, an uncertain time.

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:19.980
They were skeleton years.

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:22.640
It was a mayhem epoch.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:25.040
Around the girl, bodies were breaking.

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:27.830
Buildings and city blocks were breaking.

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:29.870
T-cells were breaking.

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:33.380
Runaway daddies left a
trail of the brokenhearted.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:37.380
Broken window was the name
of the police strategy.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:41.020
The art of the time did not
romanticize this girl's world,

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:44.850
but made something powerful
by reporting the chaos,

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:47.750
by reflecting the era's rupture.

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:51.540
To this girl, art was transformation,

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:53.590
not in the gooey kumbaya sense,

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:56.690
but in the most igniting,
conflictual sense.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:37:00.200
Art was a hammer smashing
the material world,

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:03.573
smashing the picket fence
fairy tales she saw on T.V.

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:08.890
And the girl found solace in that smashing

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:11.610
because there is
consolation in truthfulness,

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:13.810
even through the discomfort,

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:15.660
and in all that broken creation,

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:19.303
she discovered deeply
that she was not alone.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:22.730
She wasn't alone mourning
three family members

00:37:22.730 --> 00:37:24.013
felled by AIDS.

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:27.940
She wasn't alone missing
cousins who disappeared

00:37:27.940 --> 00:37:30.103
to addiction or prostitution or prison.

00:37:31.930 --> 00:37:35.573
She wasn't alone thinking she
was a zero because Daddy left.

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:39.580
She wasn't alone being blinded with rage

00:37:39.580 --> 00:37:41.633
when her cuz was blinded by a bullet.

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.940
The girl loved the
honesty of the breaking.

00:37:45.940 --> 00:37:48.953
It made new life where
there had been dereliction.

00:37:50.267 --> 00:37:52.080
Then she went to Yale.

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:54.020
Then she graduated.

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:56.160
Then her plays went to Broadway.

00:37:56.160 --> 00:37:58.250
She won prizes.

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:00.600
And it was easy to mistake the prestige,

00:38:00.600 --> 00:38:04.220
a Yale degree, international
stages, awards,

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:07.630
for moral soundness and spiritual success,

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:09.673
to think that she had done her work.

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:12.850
Some of her plays were honest.

00:38:12.850 --> 00:38:16.340
Some decent or even good,
though she was never sure.

00:38:16.340 --> 00:38:20.093
Some she knew, but didn't
admit, were too well-behaved.

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:24.293
By this time, she had
long since become a woman.

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:28.140
When the girlhood had left and
the womanhood had dropped in,

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.960
she couldn't pinpoint,

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:33.440
but during those womanhood-becoming years,

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:36.233
one cousin's HIV became undetectable.

00:38:37.090 --> 00:38:40.793
Another cousin thrived in
the long haul of recovery.

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:45.670
The blind cousin learned
braille and got a PhD.

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:48.380
Sure, prison had claimed a few

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:50.970
of the rising young generation,

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:55.380
but crack cocaine's grip
on the barrio loosened.

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:58.090
In fact, her Boriqua cousin in recovery

00:38:58.090 --> 00:39:00.670
now cooked free meals for opioid addicts

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:02.660
who were pretty much all white

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:05.640
beneath the same tracks
she used to go copping,

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:06.930
walking their city tent,

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:09.670
serving arroz con gandules
out of her car trunk,

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:12.673
thinking, there but for
the grace of God go I.

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:18.210
The woman's family was,
perhaps, less broken now.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:21.740
The less-brokenness
brought tremendous relief.

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:23.543
It brought true peace.

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:26.370
But it was easy to conflate

00:39:26.370 --> 00:39:29.290
the less brokenness with moral soundness

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.540
and spiritual success,

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:33.773
to think they had all done their work.

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:37.090
Then, one day,

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:40.690
the woman read how Corey
Menafee shattered a window,

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:44.773
decorative stained glass showing
slavery as a noble history,

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:48.620
and the little girl within her awoke,

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:50.830
the one who knew of broken things

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:54.860
And the woman, for she
had become a woman now,

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:56.780
wondered if she would be brave enough

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:59.160
to commit that simple act,

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:02.490
to break a small window at an institution

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:04.053
that she benefited from.

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:09.590
So, she took a train to New Haven

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:12.140
to attend a criminal court hearing

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:15.210
and asked Corey Menafee for permission,

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:17.863
for the honor of hearing
more and telling it.

00:40:19.500 --> 00:40:23.060
And then I, nervously, wrote
these words to read to you,

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:26.983
you room full of strangers
whom I see with love tonight,

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:30.000
and I wrote these words to Corey,

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.980
you, Corey, right there,
whom I see with love,

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:36.240
and also the complication reserved

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:38.570
for those I respect the most,

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:42.063
this is my thank-you letter
slash Aesop's fable for you.

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:47.420
The moral of the story
is that in institutions

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:49.160
that own beauty,

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:51.680
that value pedigree and decorum,

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:54.363
your truth, you, out there today,

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:57.743
your truth may be a beautiful disruption.

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:00.750
The moral of the story is that

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:03.330
there is no guidebook or checklist.

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:05.330
No great, wise leader will say, go,

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:07.700
now is the time for breaking.

00:41:07.700 --> 00:41:10.350
You are the guidebook and the checklist.

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:12.470
You are the leader.

00:41:12.470 --> 00:41:15.010
The time to disrupt is
a matter of your gut,

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:17.423
your integrity, your grace.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:21.260
The moral of the story is as you exist in

00:41:21.260 --> 00:41:23.840
and beyond Yale and carry the fraught,

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:27.400
vaunted name into true and false powers,

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:29.980
remember to exist on your own terms,

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:31.513
not on anyone else's.

00:41:32.550 --> 00:41:36.400
The moral of the story is
what was the real stain

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.670
on the stained glass window?

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:40.770
And now that the window is broken,

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:42.603
is the real stain removed?

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:49.340
The moral of the story is Corey
created an empty rectangle

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:52.350
where more light could flow.

00:41:52.350 --> 00:41:54.550
Truth through enlightenment,
(speaks Latin).

00:41:57.006 --> 00:41:59.600
By breaking Yale, he bettered Yale.

00:42:01.716 --> 00:42:02.549
The end.

00:42:03.384 --> 00:42:06.551
(audience applauding)

00:42:27.317 --> 00:42:28.998
Do you want to come up for a sec?

00:42:28.998 --> 00:42:32.165
(audience applauding)

00:42:35.877 --> 00:42:36.710
How you doing?

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:38.073
- [Corey] Thank you.

00:42:38.073 --> 00:42:42.323
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:42:47.589 --> 00:42:49.172
- Thank you, Corey.

00:42:54.005 --> 00:42:55.700
You know, I was gonna do a Q&amp;A

00:42:56.620 --> 00:42:57.840
and see if anyone had questions,

00:42:57.840 --> 00:43:02.093
and maybe, since Corey's
willing and close by,

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:05.810
would you feel comfortable
answering any questions?

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:08.590
Questions for either myself or Corey

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:11.493
could be about this topic or
about whatever's on your mind.

00:43:13.048 --> 00:43:14.410
I think there were
supposed to be microphones,

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:15.823
but I don't see them, so.

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:18.580
We have microphones.

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:20.260
Okay, great.

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:24.110
So, if anyone has an
questions, let's chat.

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:25.483
What's on people's minds?

00:43:29.320 --> 00:43:32.320
There's one microphone over
there, oh, and one here.

00:43:32.320 --> 00:43:33.740
Sandy has one.

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:34.573
Don't be shy.

00:43:34.573 --> 00:43:35.905
We all screamed together, remember?

00:43:35.905 --> 00:43:37.250
(audience laughs)

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:38.880
- [Salovey] Corey, what
are you doing right now?

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.750
- What am I doing right now?

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:44.413
Oh, I'm a head GSC at Ezra
Stiles and Morse College.

00:43:47.505 --> 00:43:48.820
In Yale Dining.

00:43:48.820 --> 00:43:50.187
(audience laughing)

00:43:50.187 --> 00:43:51.633
- Yeah, you had a question?

00:43:52.898 --> 00:43:54.367
- [Student At Right] This is a question

00:43:54.367 --> 00:43:56.043
for mostly you, Quiara.

00:43:57.440 --> 00:44:00.910
So, about a week ago, I attended
a talk with Angela Davis

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:03.630
and she talked about how
art, specifically music,

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:04.893
can be a way of healing,

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:08.170
especially in times of like burning out

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:11.660
and just like in disrupting,

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:14.680
like the emotional toll
that can have on students,

00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:18.460
how do you see art as a way of, like,

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:20.670
in your terms, of like breaking it?

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:24.300
And sort of like breaking
things that we're accustomed to

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:26.053
as a sort of like healing?

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:28.950
- So, the question was about,

00:44:28.950 --> 00:44:31.580
here, Corey, you want to come
hang at the podium with me?

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:36.580
So, the question was
about music in particular

00:44:37.080 --> 00:44:38.280
having healing qualities

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:41.270
and what's the relationship
between those healing qualities

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.870
and the disruptive qualities of art,

00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:45.703
and

00:44:48.640 --> 00:44:50.020
you know, I've thought a lot about that,

00:44:50.020 --> 00:44:52.000
and I actually was going to put in here

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:56.900
something about the healing
legacy that I come from,

00:44:56.900 --> 00:44:57.830
but I didn't because I thought

00:44:57.830 --> 00:44:59.790
it went a little bit off-topic.

00:44:59.790 --> 00:45:01.580
But I'll say this,

00:45:01.580 --> 00:45:06.000
I also come from an agricultural
and a gardening legacy,

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.390
and what I have learned from
the elder women in my family,

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:11.510
some of whom are no longer with us,

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:13.940
that they learned from their elders

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:18.410
was about the tool that
we call a hoe, right?

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:22.980
So, a hoe is, it's on like a mop handle

00:45:22.980 --> 00:45:24.510
and it's got a blade at the end of it,

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.113
and you literally break the earth, right?

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:31.350
And you breath the earth in order to

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:36.460
create troughs, narrow troughs,

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:38.233
where what goes in the troughs?

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:40.223
Seeds.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:43.270
And so I think this relationship

00:45:43.270 --> 00:45:45.000
between breaking and healing

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:46.930
isn't necessarily dichotomous.

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:50.970
I think it's symbiotic and circular,

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:55.970
and so I think the same
music that can be disruptive

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:58.230
can also be healing,

00:45:58.230 --> 00:46:00.950
and I know music has
healed me my entire life,

00:46:00.950 --> 00:46:03.080
and one of the reasons that I asked Corey

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:04.930
about what music he was
listening to as a kid

00:46:04.930 --> 00:46:06.360
because I knew it would be a way for me

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:09.330
to just understand, get into his life,

00:46:09.330 --> 00:46:12.500
get into his mind, you know?

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:13.870
And I wrote about some of the music

00:46:13.870 --> 00:46:15.823
that I was listening to, too.

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:18.330
Does that make sense?

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:24.610
- [Woman In Center] Corey, did you know

00:46:24.610 --> 00:46:26.120
what the story was today,

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:28.170
or was that the first
time you're hearing it,

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:30.690
or how do you feel about
hearing your story?

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:33.200
- Well, I was a little emotional.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:34.810
I got a little teary-eyed.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:36.111
I kind of covered it up.

00:46:36.111 --> 00:46:37.680
(audience laughing)

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:38.790
She's talking about events

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:40.400
that actually happened in my life,

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:42.380
and not so much the whole thing

00:46:42.380 --> 00:46:45.130
with the glass-breaking
thing, but prior to.

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:50.050
You know, as you live life
and you grow and develop,

00:46:50.050 --> 00:46:52.390
you tend to forget certain things

00:46:52.390 --> 00:46:55.440
and how you felt at that
time about certain things

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:56.273
that happened in your life,

00:46:56.273 --> 00:47:00.100
and just hearing her
mention Fourth of July,

00:47:00.100 --> 00:47:02.550
my family getting
together at Wharton Brook,

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:04.470
both sides of my family,
everybody in harmony,

00:47:04.470 --> 00:47:06.830
nobody arguing, nobody pointing finger,

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:08.780
everybody just having a good time.

00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:13.770
You know, that's back to
my childhood, and being 41,

00:47:13.770 --> 00:47:17.280
you tend to forget what it
was like when you were seven

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:19.317
because you get caught
up in your daily grind

00:47:19.317 --> 00:47:20.557
of this and that and that,

00:47:20.557 --> 00:47:24.140
and it was just humbling to hear her speak

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:26.390
about my childhood and different things

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:28.330
that I went through growing up.

00:47:28.330 --> 00:47:32.903
So, no I didn't know exactly
what she was gonna say,

00:47:33.790 --> 00:47:36.103
but it was good to hear.

00:47:36.969 --> 00:47:38.119
It was definitely good.

00:47:41.460 --> 00:47:42.341
- [Woman At Right] Can
you talk a little bit

00:47:42.341 --> 00:47:43.410
about the process that you went through

00:47:43.410 --> 00:47:46.510
in terms of generating this fable

00:47:46.510 --> 00:47:48.170
and just sort of like how you went about

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:49.807
asking questions and that work of it,

00:47:49.807 --> 00:47:52.670
and then also just how this relates to

00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:55.630
your work as a playwright
and how that intersects

00:47:55.630 --> 00:47:57.053
with this story?

00:47:58.370 --> 00:48:00.910
- Sure, so, well one point

00:48:00.910 --> 00:48:03.560
that Corey and I spoke about last week

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:08.030
was how the students who are
freshmen during this event

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:10.083
are now going to graduate this year,

00:48:11.310 --> 00:48:16.310
and so next year will be a
completely new group of students.

00:48:16.520 --> 00:48:17.640
We were just wondering, you know,

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:20.930
what does the story
mean when it's a totally

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:22.313
new group of students?

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:25.650
Anyway, that's a bit of a tangent,

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:29.790
but basically, as I
mentioned in the story,

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:31.310
it really blew up in the media.

00:48:31.310 --> 00:48:34.980
Corey, his lawyer Patricia is here today,

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.650
and I think she like
set him up with a phone

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:39.710
so that he could field all
these interviews at the time,

00:48:39.710 --> 00:48:41.020
and so that's how I heard about it.

00:48:41.020 --> 00:48:42.040
I heard about it in the press.

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.540
Actually, it wasn't me who heard about it.

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:45.110
It was my husband,

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:46.603
and he called me from work,

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:48.538
and he's a public defender,

00:48:48.538 --> 00:48:53.263
so he's like interested in
matters of the criminal court.

00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:55.570
So, he called me he was like,

00:48:55.570 --> 00:48:58.860
this happened, this is
gonna be your next play.

00:48:58.860 --> 00:49:00.310
This is what you have to write about.

00:49:00.310 --> 00:49:01.550
And so I was like, oh, okay.

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:03.234
So, let me check it out.

00:49:03.234 --> 00:49:04.345
(audience laughing)

00:49:04.345 --> 00:49:07.340
And so I checked it out, and he was like,

00:49:07.340 --> 00:49:09.130
the hearing is like in two days.

00:49:09.130 --> 00:49:10.390
You have to go up, you have to go up.

00:49:10.390 --> 00:49:12.800
And so I went up, and I was
like, am I allowed to go in?

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:14.833
So, he was like, yeah, you can go.

00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:19.960
You know,

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:22.320
Corey gave this interview
on Democracy Now!

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:24.360
It's a really interesting interview,

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:27.331
and that's how I started to learn,

00:49:27.331 --> 00:49:29.960
his action became a gateway
for me to learn more

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:31.460
about the history of slavery

00:49:31.460 --> 00:49:35.830
with the American college system

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:38.320
and with Ivy League schools

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:42.080
There was a scholar on there
talking about the history

00:49:42.080 --> 00:49:43.670
who was very knowledgeable on the history,

00:49:43.670 --> 00:49:45.670
so I went and read his book,

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:50.660
and I saw that interview, and
to me, Corey was magnetic.

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:53.800
I was just like, I like him.

00:49:53.800 --> 00:49:54.980
I want to meet him.

00:49:54.980 --> 00:49:55.960
I want to look him in the eye.

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:57.580
I just want to see how
this proceeding goes.

00:49:57.580 --> 00:49:58.413
So, I went up,

00:49:58.413 --> 00:50:02.260
and I've taken a little
break from playwriting now,

00:50:02.260 --> 00:50:05.350
but I was like in the thick of it then,

00:50:05.350 --> 00:50:07.260
so I was like, I was nervous.

00:50:07.260 --> 00:50:09.910
One of the first things I
talked about with Corey was

00:50:11.530 --> 00:50:13.283
I'm a light-skinned Latina.

00:50:14.590 --> 00:50:17.050
You're a black man.

00:50:17.050 --> 00:50:19.570
We're connected by this institution.

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:23.960
How do we feel about me interviewing you

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.030
and writing the story?

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:27.740
And we had those sort of conversations.

00:50:27.740 --> 00:50:31.223
Corey seemed less nervous about
that component than I was.

00:50:32.290 --> 00:50:33.570
So, we plan to write a play,

00:50:33.570 --> 00:50:38.570
and four years later, you know,

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:41.100
this is what came of those conversations.

00:50:41.100 --> 00:50:42.290
I came up to New Haven a few times.

00:50:42.290 --> 00:50:43.453
We had some meals.

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:47.310
I was really just interested
in like what his life was like,

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:48.570
just conversations.

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:52.660
The thing I love about play writing is...

00:50:52.660 --> 00:50:53.970
I always write like this,

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:55.490
but I've never done it
outside of my family.

00:50:55.490 --> 00:50:57.850
This is the first time I've
written outside of my family

00:50:57.850 --> 00:51:00.430
in terms of I'm just
sitting down Titi Jenny

00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:02.100
or Tío George and being like,

00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:04.140
so, tell me about how you
got interested in gardening,

00:51:04.140 --> 00:51:07.770
and then a whole history
of someone's life opens up

00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:10.760
in a way that would never
have in casual conversation

00:51:10.760 --> 00:51:11.940
if you didn't sit someone down and say,

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:13.560
I just want to listen
to you for a few hours.

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:14.960
Are you cool with that?

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:18.563
So, this was and still
feels new for me, you know?

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:23.010
And then I guess Corey and I can like chat

00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:24.810
on the phone tomorrow
and see what we thought

00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:28.013
and where we might want
to take it from here.

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:32.100
I have a commission from
Lincoln Center Theater,

00:51:32.100 --> 00:51:34.700
and so I thought it would be
a kind of wild topic to bring.

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:35.680
That's a very, very,

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:37.350
it's like one of the
wealthiest theater audiences

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:38.183
in New York,

00:51:38.183 --> 00:51:39.870
and I've been really nervous
to write a play for them

00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:43.210
because, I don't know.

00:51:43.210 --> 00:51:44.180
In theater, it can be very weird

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:45.550
to have like a super wealthy audience

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:47.070
watching some of the topics I deal with.

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:48.350
It feels like there's a dissonance

00:51:48.350 --> 00:51:50.060
and that can be very disturbing to me,

00:51:50.060 --> 00:51:52.120
but then I was like, maybe
this is an opportunity

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:54.730
to really, actually
engage that dissonance,

00:51:54.730 --> 00:51:57.053
and be speaking directly to that audience.

00:51:58.130 --> 00:52:01.043
Anyway, those are some
of the ramblings of it.

00:52:01.043 --> 00:52:01.876
(audience laughing)

00:52:01.876 --> 00:52:02.833
Do you have anything to add?

00:52:06.580 --> 00:52:07.863
- No, not really.

00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:16.000
It's just fascinating to me
how people are interconnected,

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:16.833
you know.

00:52:18.100 --> 00:52:19.840
Yale University is our common link,

00:52:19.840 --> 00:52:22.010
but if you read more into it,

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:24.500
we both have a lot of
similarities in our upbringing

00:52:24.500 --> 00:52:27.180
and the lives we live, you know?

00:52:29.380 --> 00:52:32.440
It's interesting to see
how people actually connect

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:33.273
with each other.

00:52:33.273 --> 00:52:35.750
Like people you would never sit down

00:52:35.750 --> 00:52:37.410
and try to have a conversation with,

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:39.277
but you got a lot in common.

00:52:39.277 --> 00:52:41.110
You just don't realize it.

00:52:41.110 --> 00:52:44.770
- I'm starting to understand
now the more I write about it

00:52:44.770 --> 00:52:46.930
what the '80s and '90s meant.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:48.740
I didn't understand it at the time.

00:52:48.740 --> 00:52:49.970
We were young.

00:52:49.970 --> 00:52:51.010
I'm 42.

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:53.150
We really did come of
age at the same time.

00:52:53.150 --> 00:52:55.313
Different cities, different neighborhoods,

00:52:56.870 --> 00:52:59.400
but part of telling the story,

00:52:59.400 --> 00:53:02.580
especially my part of the story,

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:07.580
was thinking about what
were the historic influences

00:53:08.104 --> 00:53:11.890
and trends that were affecting my life

00:53:11.890 --> 00:53:12.760
and I didn't even know it.

00:53:12.760 --> 00:53:14.850
I just thought, oh my God, why
is everything so messed up?

00:53:14.850 --> 00:53:16.720
And this isn't fair,
and that sort of thing.

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.363
That's how it felt at the time.

00:53:22.600 --> 00:53:25.060
- First I want to thank you both

00:53:25.060 --> 00:53:27.600
for an extraordinary presentation.

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:29.333
This question is for you, Corey,

00:53:30.860 --> 00:53:33.423
and I hope I can pull
together these thoughts.

00:53:34.890 --> 00:53:38.870
One of the things, one of the privileges,

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:41.750
that being here as a student at Yale,

00:53:41.750 --> 00:53:45.300
which, believe it or not, I
am as well an undergraduate,

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:50.300
confers on us is that we are
given this stamp of approval.

00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:56.120
We are given a sense that
our words are going to matter

00:53:57.010 --> 00:53:59.300
and will be paid attention to,

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:03.090
and your story, your extraordinary story,

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:07.710
will now be heard as it absolutely should.

00:54:07.710 --> 00:54:09.890
I want to ask you,

00:54:09.890 --> 00:54:14.890
if Quiara had not found you,

00:54:15.030 --> 00:54:16.940
tracked you down, sat you down.

00:54:16.940 --> 00:54:19.670
I mean, others did, Democracy
Now! and many other places,

00:54:19.670 --> 00:54:21.510
but those are very fleeting.

00:54:21.510 --> 00:54:23.603
The news today over tomorrow.

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:30.510
How would your story have been preserved

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:35.510
and did you recognize within
yourself that you're a hero?

00:54:38.490 --> 00:54:40.960
We've been trained to sort of know, oh,

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:41.793
there's the story.

00:54:41.793 --> 00:54:42.626
I'm gonna write a play.

00:54:42.626 --> 00:54:43.459
I'm gonna make a painting.

00:54:43.459 --> 00:54:44.360
I'm gonna do a mural.

00:54:44.360 --> 00:54:46.913
I'm going to write a book.

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:50.410
I find myself thinking today

00:54:50.410 --> 00:54:55.410
that the world is celebrating,
marking, mourning,

00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:58.680
the death of a 41-year-old man

00:54:58.680 --> 00:55:03.680
who is acknowledged as a
heroic African American figure,

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:09.180
and in certain ways,
that's obviously true,

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:14.180
but to me, you're the
41-year-old African American hero

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:19.550
whose contribution is enduring
in this most powerful way.

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:24.370
Would you have simply gone
back to the dining hall

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:29.370
and never felt that your story
had to go out into the world?

00:55:32.750 --> 00:55:33.583
- Well,

00:55:35.150 --> 00:55:36.698
I'm a journalism major.

00:55:36.698 --> 00:55:37.986
- [Student At Front] Okay.

00:55:37.986 --> 00:55:40.193
- So, I have writing ability.

00:55:41.340 --> 00:55:43.580
I'm also a huge procrastinator.

00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:44.900
(audience laughing)

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:45.733
So,

00:55:48.220 --> 00:55:51.960
the story is already written
in my heart and into my mind.

00:55:51.960 --> 00:55:54.210
It's just a matter of sitting down,

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:55.520
writing the story,

00:55:55.520 --> 00:55:57.282
and presenting it to the masses.

00:55:57.282 --> 00:55:58.115
- [Student At Front] But you were gonna

00:55:58.115 --> 00:55:58.948
make sure that happened, right?

00:55:58.948 --> 00:55:59.858
- Yeah, one day.

00:55:59.858 --> 00:56:01.730
(audience laughing)

00:56:01.730 --> 00:56:03.547
- He's been saying that from
the first time we talked.

00:56:03.547 --> 00:56:05.347
He was like, I want to write a book.

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:08.470
I forgot to ask you last time we spoke,

00:56:08.470 --> 00:56:11.020
but you had been talking about
potentially writing a play.

00:56:11.020 --> 00:56:13.297
- I didn't write anything.

00:56:13.297 --> 00:56:16.330
- You know, being a procrastinator
is another qualification

00:56:16.330 --> 00:56:17.267
for being a writer, so.

00:56:17.267 --> 00:56:19.820
(audience laughing)

00:56:19.820 --> 00:56:23.140
I think one more question
and then we have a reception.

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:24.530
There's gonna be a reception afterward,

00:56:24.530 --> 00:56:27.673
so we can keep the conversation going.

00:56:30.263 --> 00:56:31.096
- [Older Man In Audience]
I'd like to thank

00:56:31.096 --> 00:56:32.140
you both very much.

00:56:32.140 --> 00:56:34.610
It's a very moving presentation.

00:56:34.610 --> 00:56:38.860
My question is, the instant
that you broke the window,

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:40.510
what was going through your mind?

00:56:42.172 --> 00:56:45.250
I project on you that
it was very complicated.

00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:48.360
Or was it sort of a blind,
rash, impulsive time?

00:56:48.360 --> 00:56:50.187
- It was, I'm sorry to cut you off.

00:56:50.187 --> 00:56:51.553
It was very impulsive.

00:56:55.100 --> 00:56:55.933
You know,

00:56:58.950 --> 00:57:00.417
the reunion alum who pointed it out to me,

00:57:00.417 --> 00:57:02.913
that happened like a Sunday,

00:57:04.380 --> 00:57:08.163
and I came back to work Monday,

00:57:09.020 --> 00:57:10.750
and I did it on Tuesday,

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:11.933
so it was...

00:57:16.050 --> 00:57:18.920
That sounds fast, but
it was a couple of days

00:57:18.920 --> 00:57:20.130
where it was like...

00:57:22.140 --> 00:57:23.470
I don't know.

00:57:23.470 --> 00:57:25.340
I don't want to sound like I'm crazy,

00:57:25.340 --> 00:57:29.543
but in your mind, you go through
the should I, should I not,

00:57:30.520 --> 00:57:33.520
and in my mind,

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:35.610
I always convince myself,
no, don't be stupid.

00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:37.000
Why would you, no.

00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:38.243
Just no.

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:40.143
But,

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:44.003
in that moment, something just overcame.

00:57:46.540 --> 00:57:51.540
We had a 10-minute
break from nine to 9:10,

00:57:52.100 --> 00:57:56.213
and it was in that time,
I just was like, you know,

00:57:57.160 --> 00:57:58.714
yeah, that thing needs to come down.

00:57:58.714 --> 00:57:59.840
(audience laughing)

00:57:59.840 --> 00:58:01.433
And I just did it impulsively.

00:58:04.000 --> 00:58:06.150
I obviously didn't think about my children

00:58:06.150 --> 00:58:07.550
or my own wellbeing,

00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:11.000
because if I had, I probably
would not have done that.

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:11.833
But,

00:58:15.460 --> 00:58:16.800
I have a lot of knowledge,

00:58:16.800 --> 00:58:20.603
but I don't always go off
of the, what's the word,

00:58:21.550 --> 00:58:23.820
the intelligence of a situation.

00:58:23.820 --> 00:58:26.223
Sometimes I go off the heart feeling.

00:58:27.430 --> 00:58:28.380
How does it make me feel?

00:58:28.380 --> 00:58:31.560
What do I really need
to do in this situation?

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:35.730
You know, sometimes
situations call for you not

00:58:35.730 --> 00:58:37.370
to do the most logical thing

00:58:38.350 --> 00:58:41.790
in order to accomplish things.

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:43.030
That was one instance,

00:58:43.030 --> 00:58:43.863
and it just happened to work out for me.

00:58:43.863 --> 00:58:45.450
It could have been disastrous.

00:58:45.450 --> 00:58:48.769
I could be on the street
begging for money right now.

00:58:48.769 --> 00:58:50.100
You never know.

00:58:50.100 --> 00:58:51.527
But, God had my back,

00:58:51.527 --> 00:58:54.377
and he made sure everything
worked out for me,

00:58:54.377 --> 00:58:56.653
and to him, I'm most grateful.

00:58:58.340 --> 00:59:01.220
- I think that's a beautiful
place, actually, to end,

00:59:01.220 --> 00:59:05.800
and keep the conversation
going out in the reception.

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:06.633
So, thank you all.

00:59:06.633 --> 00:59:07.486
Thank you, Corey.

00:59:07.486 --> 00:59:10.653
(audience applauding)

00:59:19.722 --> 00:59:23.222
(bright electronic music)

